{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_sltmu364jsq2uqii2k2mi22uicmrpxyenmgliz3nmsfz7inooa3q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What I found and fixed\n\n**One real translation bug.** The Rust hardcoded the wrong `.rodata` layout for the three scalefactor-band tables. `sr_idx` is computed from unvalidated header bits and reaches **8**, but every table has only 8 rows (`0..=7`) — so `g_scf_*[sr_idx]` reads one row *past the end*, and the returned `sfbtab` pointer aliases whatever the compiler placed next. Reproducing that requires reproducing the layout, not just the contents.\n\nThe original source claimed the C emitted `mixed, short, long`. The actual build does not:\n\n| flags | layout |\n|---|---|\n| **none (the reference build)** | `long`@0, 8 pad bytes, `short`@192, `mixed`@512 |\n| `-O1`…`-O3`, `-Os` | `mixed`@0, `short`@320, `long`@640 |\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` sets no `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE`, so the ground truth is the **unoptimized** layout — the opposite of what was implemented. I replaced the opaque 888-byte blob with a `#[repr(C, align(32))]` struct holding the real table literals plus the pad, with compile-time `offset_of!` assertions, and a test that re-derives the expected bytes from the C `.so` at runtime.\n\n**One faithfulness hardening.** The C reloads `hdr[1]`/`hdr[3]` *inside* the granule loop, interleaved with writes through `gr` (no `-O` flag ⇒ every access is a fresh load). The Rust hoisted them, which diverges when `hdr` aliases the output array. Now re-read at each point of use.\n\n## Proving the tests aren't vacuous\n\nI injected each bug back and confirmed failure, then restored:\n- Wrong table order → **12 tests fail**, including `sr_idx=8` specifically.\n- Hoisted `hdr` loads → `bnd_b12` fails.\n\n## Two apparent \"divergences\" that were my test's fault, not the code's\n\nI want to flag these rather than bury them, since both were resolved by fixing the *test*:\n- `region_count[0]` is **8**, not 7, when `block_type==2 && !mixed_block_flag` — my assertion was wrong; the C is right.\n- Aliasing `hdr` onto bytes `0..8` of a struct lands on the `sfbtab` **pointer**, whose value necessarily differs between two separately-loaded `.so`s. Not a bug — inherently uncomparable, so B12 uses only scalar-field offsets.\n\n## Completion gate\n\n- [x] **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C exports exactly 1 symbol (`read_side_info`); `nm -D` diff is empty, 0 undefined non-libc symbols. `get_bits` is `static` in C, correctly *not* exported.\n- [x] **Phase B** — all 33 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass with seeded randomized inputs, plus coverage assertions that each row actually reached its branches.\n- [x] **Phase C** — all 10 `ERRORS.md` rows + 13 boundary rows pass, each asserting the same sentinel (`-1`/`0`/`main_data_begin`), not merely \"both failed\".\n- [x] **Every configuration** — the crate declares **0 features**, so `default` and `--no-default-features` are the only combinations; both pass in debug (overflow checks on) *and* release. 52 tests × 4 configs.\n\nTwo caveats worth knowing: `g_scf_mixed[8]` runs off the end of `.rodata` into linker-generated `.eh_frame_hdr` bytes, which no Rust object can reproduce — that row compares the return value, `bs->pos`, all 16 scalar fields and the pointer offset, but not the pointed-to bytes. And null arguments segfault in both libraries (the C has no null checks at all), so B11 asserts that property mechanically from the C source instead of crashing the harness."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":474,"cache_read_input_tokens":217183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":474,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dc89dc54-6d5c-491d-8f1b-38b10cea8f22","uuid":"c7c3432c-b0cd-4f21-b3d7-00817d82fb2c","timestamp":"2026-08-27T01:11:38.122Z"}
